{
  "id": "Compound::DB00445 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Epirubicin is implicated in the pathogenesis of hematologic cancer through a series of interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Epirubicin may contribute to the development of hematologic cancer via a pathway involving anthracycline antineoplastic antibiotics, as it is linked to myelodysplasia and urethral obstruction, both of which are associated with doxorubicin. These conditions, in turn, are linked to hematologic cancer, indicating a potential role of Epirubicin in the progression of the disease. The shared ancestry with doxorubicin, which is a well-known anthracycline antibiotic with established antineoplastic activity, supports the notion that Epirubicin may exert similar effects. Additionally, the presence of a common ancestor in the class of anthracycline antineoplastic antibiotics underscores the biological relevance of this relationship. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link between Epirubicin and hematologic cancer, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
